<Base title="About" description="Who AVI is, how it works, and how it is funded.">
  <h1>About AVI</h1>

  <p>
    Founded in 2016, the Association of Vapers India (AVI) is now in its
    second decade as India's leading consumer advocacy voice for tobacco
    harm reduction (THR) — founded by former smokers who quit through
    vaping, and working for the right of India's tobacco users to access
    lower-risk alternatives to combustible tobacco. Our focus has expanded
    to include smokeless nicotine products — nicotine pouches and snus —
    given that an estimated 200 million Indians use smokeless tobacco,
    roughly twice the number who smoke.
  </p>

  <p>
    Over ten years, AVI has built a track record few consumer bodies
    anywhere in the Global South can match: it has organised and fought
    litigation across more Indian courts than any other THR advocacy
    group, built a citizen movement that has taken its case directly to
    government and the public, and helped carry India's experience — good
    and bad — onto the international stage, where restrictive,
    Western-designed tobacco-control models are too often exported to
    lower-income countries without regard for local reality. See our
    <a href="/litigation">litigation</a> and
    <a href="/press">press coverage</a> for the documented record of this
    work, and our <a href="/testimonials">testimonials</a> page for
    accounts from the Indians this work has been for all along.
  </p>

  <p>
    AVI was founded by <strong>Samrat Chowdhery</strong>, who continues to
    direct the organisation. Samrat is the past president of the
    International Network of Nicotine Consumer Organisations (INNCO), which
    represents advocacy bodies across dozens of countries with UN observer
    status, and has represented Indian and Global South consumers at
    international forums including the Global Forum on Nicotine and the WHO
    Framework Convention on Tobacco Control conferences — work that has
    repeatedly put Indian THR advocacy, rather than only Western
    experience, in front of global policymakers.
  </p>

